# .bulwark.yml — this file is yours.
#
# gt created it once and will never modify or delete it again. bulwark owns
# what goes in it; gt only makes sure it exists, because the one setting below
# decides whether the pipeline runs your test suite once or twice.
#
# `coverage.source` says who produces the coverage bulwark gates on:
#
#   run     bulwark executes the suite itself. Slower, and correct whenever
#           nothing else has produced a report yet.
#   report  bulwark only reads a report someone else produced. Faster, and
#           WRONG until something actually produces one — bulwark exits with
#           an error when told to read a report that is not there.
#
# It starts as `run` because that is what is true the moment this file is
# created: ci-test is a scaffolded no-op, so nothing uploads coverage yet.
#
# Change it to `report` in the same commit that makes ci-test produce coverage
# and upload it as the `gt-coverage` artifact, which the bulwark stage extracts
# before bulwark runs. Flipping it earlier trades a double test run for a
# failing security gate.
#
# Lay that artifact out the way the repository is laid out. bulwark looks for
# each report next to the unit that produced it — `<moduleDir>/coverage.out` per
# Go module, `<crateDir>/coverage/` per Rust crate, and
# `<packageDir>/coverage/{coverage-summary.json,lcov.info}` per TypeScript
# package, the last of which has no path override because those locations are
# Vitest convention rather than a project's choice. The bulwark stage mirrors
# the artifact into the scan root, so an artifact whose paths match the repo's
# needs no further configuration, and a single-module repo can still upload one
# `coverage.out` at the top level.
#
# Everything else bulwark supports — per-language enablement, coverage
# tolerances, patch-coverage opt-outs, toolchain overrides — belongs here too.
# See bulwark's README.
coverage:
  source: run
